Unreleased
---
Feature
- QxTranslator::translate() looks a string up in the catalogues this object
  installed. The class had languageChanged() and said in its own
  documentation that "a slot may call translate() immediately" - but there was
  no such call: a model, a controller or a log writer holding a QxTranslator
  had to reach for QCoreApplication and know that the two were connected. The
  lookup is Qt's, forwarded, so this is not a second answer to the question;
  what is new is that it is reachable from the object that owns the answer, and
  that it carries the count through to the plural form.
  tst_QxTranslator::translateFollowsTheInstalledCatalogue
- QxSettings reads and writes a whole group at once: section(prefix) returns its
  keys with the prefix taken off, setSection(prefix, values) writes them. The
  class had beginGroup()/endGroup(), which pair up by hand - an early return
  between them leaves the group open for every later call - while the one thing
  a restore path wants is the whole group in one step. An empty prefix means the
  group already in force, so a caller's own beginGroup() is never cancelled.
  childGroups() and childKeys() join allKeys() as the enumeration half.
  tst_QxSettings::sections

Bugfix
- QxSettings::setVersion() was the one write that reached QSettings without a
  word, so a listener holding a copy of the settings could not learn about it.
  It now reports through valueChanged() like every other write. No caller
  depends on the silence: the version is a stored key, not a private field.

0.1.3
---
Bugfix
- Rotating the log file dropped the result of reopening it. If that reopen
  failed, the logger went on running with no file behind it and every later line
  was lost, and nothing said so - the rotation has already moved the old file
  away at that point, so there is no way back from it. The failure is now
  reported on stderr, which is the only channel left to a logger that cannot use
  qWarning() without re-entering itself. The failure cannot be forced from a
  test portably - it takes a file system that refuses the file - so
  tst_QxLogger::keepsItsFileAfterRotation pins the other half: a rotation that
  works still leaves a file behind the logger.

0.1.2
---
Bugfix
- QX_CORE_DEPRECATED_SINCE(major, minor) never answered true for a deprecation
  that had already happened: QX_CORE_DISABLE_DEPRECATED_BEFORE defaulted to
  QX_CORE_VERSION, so the deprecation point was compared against the version being
  built - (0,1,0) > (0,1,0) is false - and the answer flipped back to false on
  every minor bump. The default is now a fixed floor, so an API stays declared for
  as long as it is inside its removal buffer (K26), and raising the cutoff is what
  compiles it out. tst_qxdeprecated covers all seven libraries
